  [1mTestability shapes the code.[0m [38;5;153mtrack[39m resolves [38;5;153mfetch[39m off [38;5;153mglobalThis[39m at call time rather than capturing it at import, so
  tests substitute a fake with no dependency-injection plumbing. Worth covering: identity attaches after [38;5;153midentify[39m;
  [38;5;153muserId[39m is [38;5;153mnull[39m without it; [38;5;153mreset[39m clears; non-ok and rejected responses log without throwing; [38;5;153mtrack[39m returns
  synchronously.

  Keeping all logic in [38;5;153mtracking.js[39m means the part that [3misn't[0m unit-testable — the DOM wiring in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m — stays thin
  enough not to need it.

  [1mRunning the page.[0m Module scripts don't load from [38;5;153mfile://[39m, so opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m directly stops working. It'll need
  [38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m or equivalent. I'll note this in the README as part of the change.

  [1mTooling.[0m This repo has none configured — no linter, no formatter, no test runner, no scripts in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m. It's
  cheapest to add now, before there's code to retrofit. My recommendation is unit tests via Node's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m
  and [38;5;153mnode:assert[39m (zero dependencies, runs ESM natively, preserves the repo's current zero-dep state) plus a formatter.
  I'd skip end-to-end: it needs a browser driver and real dependencies to cover one form.

  Whatever you pick goes into the spec's Global Constraints so the implementation plan inherits it.
